Brain Stroke Dataset: Decision Tree Prediction Report

I. Overview

This document reports summaries and interpretations on factors identified as statistically significant to brain stroke 
using results from three different decision tree models. The models include original, balanced, and tuned decision trees, 
where the two latter ones are modified from the original tree model to enhance performance metrics. The final model, tuned 
decision tree model, was selected for best interpretability and prediction performance. The first three sections each show model 
summary tables, variable importance, accuracy tests, ROC Curve, and decision tree plot obtained using two programming languages, 
R and Python. The document ends with an interpretation of major statistics and overall conclusion on the significant predictors 
of stroke.


II. Data

Dataset  |	 Observations	  Stroke Cases	 Stroke Rate (%)
-----------------------------------------------------------
Original |      4891	         248	           4.98%
Train	   |     2,491	         123	           4.94%
Test	   |     2,490	         125	           5.02%

	The original patient dataset was split into 50% training set and 50% testing set using random data partitioning functions in R and Python libraries.
	The splitting algorithm ensured stroke rate was approximately similar between training and testing set.
	Only the training set was used for constructing tree models.
	The testing set was used to obtain predicted stroke probabilities and measure overall model performance.


III. Decision Tree Models

#1 Original Model
Software used: R Studio, Spyder (Python)
Training time:	0.0566 seconds

Summary
Formula:	stroke ~ Gender + age + hypertension + heart_disease + ever_married + work_type + Residence_type + avg_glucose_level + bmi + smoking_status
Method:	class
Training Observations (n):	2491
CP (Complexity Parameter):	0.008130081
Number of Splits (nsplit):	0
Relative Error (rel error):	1.0
Cross-Validation Error (xerror):	0
Cross-Validation Std Dev (xstd):	0
Predicted Class:	0
Expected Loss:	0.04937776
Class Counts:	2368 non-stroke, 123 stroke
Class Probabilities:	0.951 non-stroke, 0.049 stroke
Variable Importance:	NULL

Key Observations
	The tree did not branch/split at all
  	This indicates that the dataset is highly imbalanced
    	2368 non-stroke cases  ~95%
    	123 stroke cases  ~5% stroke prevalence
    	Tree likely minimizes error by predicting No Stroke all the time
    	This corresponds with the probabilities listed (0.951 non-stroke, 0.049 stroke)
  	No split that reduces impurity
	Model predicts the majority class (“No Stroke”) for every patient
	Since no splits occurred, no variables used  variable importance is NULL
	The class imbalance is severe enough that a standard decision tree cannot detect stroke patterns effectively.

Accuracy Test (Python & R)
Threshold: 0.5
	Test Accuracy: 0.947
	Sensitivity: 0.0161
	Specificity: 0.9958
	Confusion Matrix:
              Actual
Predicted	   0	     1
    0	      2357	   10
    1	       122	   2


#2 Balanced Tree Model
Software used: R Studio
Feature: Class Balancing
  	Created balanced training data (50% stroke, 50% non-stroke cases)
  	Use it to build a new tree model

Summary Table
