Complexity Parameter (CP) Table
CP	           Number of Splits	      Relative Error	  Cross-Validation Error (xerror)	      xstd
0.56910569	            0	               1.0000000	              1.1788618	                0.06272953
0.03252033	            1	               0.4308943	              0.4634146	                0.05380166
0.01000000	            3	               0.3658537	              0.4471545	                0.05312823

Key Observations
	Age is the strongest predictor 
  	 Importance score: ~50
  	Root node (greatest reduction in classification impurity
  	Patients younger than ~48 years were mostly classified as non-stroke.
  	This supports our earlier logistic regression findings where age had the strongest statistical significance.
	Hypertension emerges as important predictor
  	Appears in later branches (Node 3, Node 6)
  	Node 3 contained older patients, higher hypertension prevalence, higher BMI/glucose
  	This suggests hypertension interacts with age in determining stroke risk, which aligns with the interaction logistic regression model.
	BMI contributed moderately in refining the predictions (adds strength to age:bmi interaction term)
	Work type and Marriage status were identified as meaningful classification variables
	Overall, there is a consistency observed across models in identifying significant factors

